{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788355321,"nonce":"ej97teCYt2y0Mjm1nm8JTY0U9BE=","message":"**MERGED, NOT YET CLOSED — orchestrator verification, repair round 2026-09-02.** Commit `192964c` on `main` (rebased, fast-forwarded); assembly rebuilt as the standalone `4491159`. **Held `state:doing` pending the bench** — this is item 2 of the round's acceptance list and no bench has run.\n\nBoth mechanisms ship distinctly, as the widened acceptance required: `enclosed` (`ProperRoom`) and `uses_outdoor_temp` (`UsesOutdoorTemperature`), aggregated over the layout's interior rooms and republished per room. Surfaced on `construction {layout_id}.enclosure`, `rooms.layouts_unenclosed` and `digest.construction.layouts_unenclosed`.\n\n\n`RimWorld/ThingDefGenerator_Buildings.NewFrameDef_Thing` does:\n\n```csharp\nthingDef.building.isEdifice = def.building.isEdifice;\n```\n\n**So a wall FRAME is an edifice and registers in `EdificeGrid`**, while the same generator clamps `passability` to `PassThroughOnly` and sets `fillPercent = 0.2f`. Keying the gap check on \"an edifice stands at this cell\" would therefore have reported a half-built wall as sealed — the exact false-clean this issue exists to end. The check calls `Verse/RegionTypeUtility.GetExpectedRegionType` instead: a declared shell cell closes the room exactly when it answers `None` or `Portal`. The issue's own nuance — *a door does not break enclosure, an unbuilt door does* — then falls out of the mechanism rather than being special-cased. Confirmed in the decompile at line 213.\n\n\n1. **The IR roof mask is not held by the mod.** `place-layout` takes a resolved element list, and `LayoutVerbs`' header records the roof as a deliberate non-consumption; adding a `roof` argument would change another issue's verb contract. \"Intended roofed\" is the *declared interior* instead — the same set for a room layout, no new IR field, and it is what `UsesOutdoorTemperature` counts against. Where the built room is larger, `OpenRoofCount` is published beside the named holes with a note saying which is the floor. **This is a weaker form than the acceptance text implies** and is called out as such here.\n2. **\"Intends a room\" is decided from the declaration alone** — a 4-connected flood over the layout's own rect blocked by declared `Wall`/`Door` cells, with no game state in it. That is the cry-wolf guard: a defensive wall gets `enclosed: null`, never `false`, and never appears in a roll-up.\n\nAcceptance item 4 (consistency with `f9dadc7`'s stalled roll-up) was met by adopting that issue's settled field idiom in advance — `unenclosed_for {since_tick, ticks, day_boundaries, tracked_since, stale}`, in memory, cleared in `Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary`, no scribed state. **`f9dadc7` is still in flight, so if it lands with different field names one of the two has to move.**\n\nOrchestrator checks re-run independently: `dotnet build -c Release` on the rebased branch and again on `main` (0/0); `accept/a1644d6-enclosure.py --selftest` -\u003e **16/16 PASS**. Checks 9.1a-e are the strongest part: they take a banked envelope from the run itself, show `eq(...,None)` PASSES on the absent enclosure key, show `shape()` FAILS on it, and show the run read `done:true` while the freezer was the great outdoors.\n\n\n- **No bench was touched.** The `enclosure` block shape, `layouts_unenclosed` on `rooms` and `digest`, the named gap cells, `unenclosed_for`, and the roof flip are all unproven against a running game.\n- **Observer safety is argued, not measured.** That `GetExpectedRegionType`, `GetRoom`, `Roofed`, `ProperRoom` and `UsesOutdoorTemperature` mutate nothing scribed is read off the decompile.\n- **Cost is unmeasured.** `digest` now carries an enclosure scan and `digest` is documented as called constantly. It is capped at 8 layouts sharing one 4096-cell budget and emits only failing rows, but nobody has timed it on a 38-mod bench. **This is the one thing to watch during bench acceptance.**\n\n\nThe freezer template is checkable only because `Cooler` is `fillPercent 1.0` / `Impassable`. Had it been partial-fillage, the declared-interior flood would escape through the north wall and **the very template that shipped this failure would have been silently exempt from the check.**","files":null}]}